19.4  nftables vs iptables vs ufw

TL;DR
  - The kernel does the filtering (netfilter). iptables, nftables, ufw and
    firewalld are ways to write its rules.
  - nftables (nft) replaced iptables. On current distros, the iptables command
    often writes nftables rules underneath.
  - ufw (Ubuntu) and firewalld (Fedora, RHEL) are simpler front-ends: use the
    one your distro ships, and only that one.

      sudo ufw status verbose
      sudo firewall-cmd --list-all
      sudo nft list ruleset

On your machine
  firewalld inactive
  ufw       active
  nftables  inactive
  ufw.conf  ENABLED=yes

Pitfalls
  - Several tools at once write rules that fight each other: pick one front-end.
  - Over SSH, allow SSH before turning a firewall on (sudo ufw allow OpenSSH),
    or you lock yourself out.
  - Docker writes its own rules, so ports it publishes bypass ufw (see 19.1).
  - Rules added by hand with nft or iptables vanish at reboot unless your
    distro's service saves them.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why did nftables replace iptables?

    iptables needed separate tools for IPv4, IPv6, ARP and bridges, and replaced
    its whole rule table for every change. nftables has one tool and one syntax
    for all of them, sets and maps for long lists of addresses, and atomic
    updates. It arrived in kernel 3.13 and became the default in Debian 10 and
    RHEL 8.

  ▾ What are firewalld zones?

    firewalld groups interfaces and networks into zones (public, home, trusted),
    each with its own rules: a laptop on café Wi-Fi sits in public. Changes made
    without --permanent are lost at the next reload, which makes it easy to try
    a rule first.

Related
  Commands  nft, ufw, firewall-cmd
  Paths     /etc/nftables.conf, /etc/ufw/
  Hub       atfq network Network
  See also  atfq 19.1 Who listens on which port
  See also  atfq 19.3 Interfaces and routes
